d3d10umd: run-time indexes one at a time, through ADDR[0]

The translator handed out ADDR[0], ADDR[1] and ADDR[2] in turn for run-time indexes, but
virglrenderer writes every indexed store to a temporary array through addr0, and 0.10.4 also
reads indexed constants through addr0, whatever register the shader names; it drops the
constant part of a run-time geometry shader vertex index, ignores a run-time index on a pixel
shader's colour outputs, and cannot index immediates. In WinSAT's ALU and texture tests (about
4100 shaders each, with run-time constant reads) the second read of a shader fetched the first
one's element on the rig.

Now each indexed read is a MOV of its own right after the UARL that sets ADDR[0]; each indexed
write goes to a temporary that is stored through ADDR[0] after the instruction, or for a
pixel shader's colour output, under a compare per declared output; a geometry shader's vertex
index has its constant part added in and goes through ADDR[1]; the immediate constant buffer
is read through selects up to 16 entries and through a temporary array copy up to 64, with
zero past its end as Direct3D reads past a constant buffer (beyond 64 entries such reads are
still wrong, and logged). The instructions with two results (SINCOS, UDIV, IMUL, UMUL) take
all their operands before writing either result, so neither a source nor a run-time index is
read after the other result changed it.

